    <h1>What Is a Raven's Favourite Food?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens strongly prefer meat — particularly large-animal carrion, organ meat, brain, and eggs. Their bill is adapted to tear through carcass skin and open eggs. When choice is available, ravens consistently favour high-protein, high-fat animal food over plant material. Carrion from ungulates (deer, moose, cattle) is the most important food type for populations in northern and montane environments.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Preferred Food Types</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens show clear food preferences when multiple options are available:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> ungulate carcasses (deer, moose, elk, caribou, cattle, sheep) are the most important food source for ravens in northern environments. Ravens are attracted to carcasses from several kilometres and can consume large amounts quickly. They prioritise organ meat, fat deposits, and brain — the most energy-dense parts — before consuming muscle.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> a highly preferred seasonal food; ravens actively seek bird nests during the spring breeding season.</li>
      <li><strong>Stored (cached) food:</strong> ravens revisit caches of meat and fat preferentially, suggesting awareness of the high value of stored animal protein.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> in areas with human settlement, ravens exploit high-calorie human food — especially meat scraps, fat, and cooked food. This preference for human waste is a major driver of raven population increase near urban areas.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Seasonal Dietary Shifts</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> While ravens consistently prefer meat, their actual diet shifts seasonally based on what is available:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Winter:</strong> carrion-dependent; ungulate die-offs and cached food are critical. Ravens will follow wolves, coyotes, and human hunters to access carcasses.</li>
      <li><strong>Spring:</strong> eggs and nestlings; invertebrates emerging; some plant material (early berries in warmer climates).</li>
      <li><strong>Summer:</strong> invertebrates (beetles, earthworms, insect larvae); fruits and berries; small mammals; some ground-nesting bird predation continues.</li>
      <li><strong>Autumn:</strong> caching season — ravens invest in storing high-calorie foods (nuts, meat, fat) for winter retrieval. Berry and fruit consumption peaks where seasonal abundance allows.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Captive Food Preferences</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> In captive studies and zoo settings, food preferences can be directly tested by offering ravens simultaneous choices. Results consistently show:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Strong preference for meat over equivalent volumes of plant food.</li>
      <li>Preference for fat-rich over lean meat.</li>
      <li>Preference for novel foods when familiar staples are well-supplied (neophilia toward food, in contrast to neophobia toward novel objects in general).</li>
      <li>Willingness to defer consumption of a preferred food to cache it for later — suggesting they value future access to preferred food.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

